<h2>One material for meshes and terrain</h2>
<p>Create <strong>Material</strong> from the Assets panel and open its source to edit the surface. A material has one stable <code>asset://</code> ID. Assign that same ID to a Mesh Renderer, a terrain biome override, or a role in a terrain material set. There is no separate Terrain Material asset type.</p>
<p>The integrated material document provides map pickers, surface settings, render state, Undo/Redo and Save. Its color swatch shows the base tint; inspect actual mapped shading on a scene object or terrain. Import texture assets before assigning them. Map pickers also accept RenderTarget assets; these use their live texture contents rather than generated image mipmaps. Material import and reimport validate their references and record texture and shader dependencies for cooking.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "format_version": 1,
  "mapping": "triplanar",
  "textures": {
    "albedo": "asset://surfaces/rock/color",
    "normal": "asset://surfaces/rock/normal",
    "roughness": "asset://surfaces/rock/roughness"
  },
  "parameters": {
    "tiling": 0.5,
    "normal_strength": 0.8
  }
}</code></pre>
<p>These IDs illustrate the format; select assets installed in your project. The default shader is <code>builtin://lit</code>. Omit unused maps and default settings. <code>parameters.base_color</code> is an sRGB RGBA tint, default white; the mesh or biome tint still multiplies it. Roughness defaults to 0.8 and metallic to 0. Values for either scalar are in 0–1.</p>
<h3>Map meanings</h3>
<table><thead><tr><th>Texture key</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>albedo</code></td><td>sRGB base RGB and linear alpha.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>normal</code></td><td>Linear RGB tangent-space normal. <code>normal_strength</code> defaults to 1; zero removes its perturbation.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>roughness</code></td><td>Linear red channel; dark is smooth and bright is rough.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>metallic</code></td><td>Linear red channel; zero is dielectric, one is metallic.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ambient_occlusion</code></td><td>Linear red channel; reduces ambient illumination, not direct sun visibility.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>detail_normal</code></td><td>Additional tangent-space normal controlled by <code>detail_strength</code>, default 0. Assign the map before increasing its strength.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>emissive</code></td><td>sRGB emission color. This does not illuminate nearby geometry.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>height</code></td><td>Linear red channel biases existing terrain layer weights through <code>height_blend_strength</code>. Ordinary mesh shaders do not sample it. It does not displace vertices or change collision.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Color mipmaps average in linear light before returning to sRGB storage. Normal mipmaps renormalize averaged vectors; scalar maps average linear values. Color image variants use hardware sRGB decoding before bilinear and mip-level filtering; alpha remains linear. Scalar and normal variants do not decode their channels. Live scene RenderTargets contain display-sRGB in raw storage: color slots decode those contents once in the shader, after their existing raw texture filtering. A source used for different purposes can have separate GPU sampling variants, so interpreting a normal map does not change a UI texture using the same image.</p>
<h3>Mapping and normal conventions</h3>
<p><code>mapping</code> is <code>uv</code> by default and uses ordinary mesh texture coordinates; terrain uses its continuous local X/Z coordinates. <code>triplanar</code> projects onto three local surface axes and blends them by surface orientation, avoiding the stretched top-down projection on cliffs. <code>parameters.tiling</code> is positive, defaults to 1, and increases repetition when raised. In UV mode it multiplies the mesh UVs; in triplanar mode it specifies repeats per local surface unit. Transform scale therefore changes the world size of the pattern.</p>
<p>Texture wrapping remains an import setting. Use <code>repeat</code> on tileable terrain maps; clamping a map intentionally stretches its edge outside its authored range. Triplanar projection does not make a non-tileable source seamless.</p>
<p><code>normal_map_convention</code> is <code>opengl</code> by default (+Y/green up). Choose <code>directx</code> for −Y/green down maps. This describes the source image convention, independently of the rendering backend or operating system. The normal and detail-normal maps use the same convention.</p>
<h3>Terrain layer controls</h3>
<p>Shared Material parameters include <code>height_blend_strength</code> (default 0.5, range 0–1), <code>macro_variation_strength</code> (default 0, range 0–1), and <code>macro_variation_scale</code> (default 0.02, positive cycles per terrain-local unit). These control terrain layer shading and do not change mesh geometry, erosion or collision. Height strength biases normalized layer weights using the assigned linear height map; a missing height map is neutral and strength zero preserves the original weights. Macro variation darkens albedo with deterministic position-based noise, reducing visible uniform repetition; strength zero disables it. These controls do not add geometric displacement or make non-tileable textures seamless. The ordinary mesh material path retains them without applying terrain layer blending.</p>
<h3>Terrain integration and current limits</h3>
<p>Terrain material sets bind automatic roles such as ground, rock, wet ground and snow to ordinary Material IDs. Classification uses the generated terrain’s slope, erosion transport, moisture and water coverage. Set biome <code>allow_snow: false</code> to exclude automatic snow; its default is true. An explicit biome Material overrides automatic roles, and the biome color remains a tint.</p>
<p>Compatible opaque terrain uses instanced GPU patches with five material arrays: albedo, normal, packed ambient-occlusion/roughness/metallic/height, detail normal and emissive. These support mapped PBR surfaces, triplanar projection, height-biased layer weights and macro variation. The renderer decodes exact material IDs before blending the four neighboring field samples; four contributors per cell are not a four-role limit on the source material set. <code>Terrain3D.surface_blending</code> applies to mapped and solid materials: enabled interpolates contributors; disabled selects the original triangle’s material.</p>
<p>The mapped terrain path requires 12 texture sampler slots, texture-array support, vertex texture sampling and instancing. Actual device texture dimensions, array-layer limits and configured memory budgets constrain residency. Custom shaders, transparency/cutout, unsupported devices, nearest or mip-disabled image sampling, incompatible map wrapping or live RenderTarget maps use the ordinary mesh path with an explicit fallback reason. Array maps require linear filtering and enabled mipmaps. All image maps within one terrain Material must share one wrap mode; different Materials may use repeat, clamp or mirror independently. Maps are not silently dropped to keep GPU batching.</p>
<p>The ordinary mesh path renders individual PBR materials with discrete triangle assignments. Terrain height-weight blending and macro variation belong to the GPU layer path; they do not alter geometry or collision. Ordinary mapped meshes require nine sampler slots and report unsupported capabilities. Array tiers and detail-normal fade use the shared <a href="/docs/terrain">Environment3D terrain quality settings</a>. These contracts describe the implemented path; they do not establish platform or visual qualification.</p>
<h3>Starter terrain materials</h3>
<p>Install <a href="/packages/polyhaven.materials.terrain">Poly Haven Terrain Materials</a> with <code>demi package add polyhaven.materials.terrain@^1.0.0</code>. It supplies five CC0 1K source surfaces, a wet-gravel variant, physical scales, provenance and an alpine material set/palette. Use <code>asset://polyhaven/terrain/palettes/alpine</code> as the terrain recipe's palette, or assign individual Material IDs to meshes and biome overrides. These are real source maps, not geometry or vegetation generators.</p>
<h3>Migrating the retired terrain material format</h3>
<p>The former <code>DataAsset</code> content type <code>terrain_material</code> is rejected with migration guidance. Keep the stable ID, change the manifest to <code>type: Material</code> with importer <code>material</code>, and retain <code>format_version</code> in both documents. Remove obsolete data-specific <code>content_type</code>/<code>schema</code> settings and unused schema dependencies. Reimport to refresh dependencies. Regenerate derived terrain and recook older outputs; the prepared terrain payload is now version 9.</p>
<p>Move scalar surface settings into <code>parameters</code>; convert the old linear <code>base_color</code> RGB to sRGB (alpha stays linear). Replace the <code>maps</code> array with a <code>textures</code> object and rename <code>base_color</code> to <code>albedo</code>. Replace <code>triplanar: true</code> with <code>mapping: triplanar</code>. Use <code>detail_normal</code> only for an actual normal map; the retired generic detail slot did not establish that meaning. Terrain material sets and palettes remain typed data assets.</p>
